1) Changing the kernel size of the first convolutional layer: By changing the kernel size of the first convolutional layer, we can adjust the amount of information preserved during down sampling. A larger kernel size will result in less information being lost but also requires more computation power. On the other hand, a smaller kernel size will lead to faster inference times but may sacrifice accuracy due to reduced capacity.

2) Using different activation functions: Different activation functions can affect the behavior of neural networks significantly. For instance, ReLU activations tend to produce sparser representations compared to tanh activations. Therefore, experimenting with alternative activation functions could yield better results depending upon the specific task at hand.

3) Adding dropout layers: Dropout layers introduce randomness into the training process which helps prevent overfitting. They work best when combined with regularization techniques like L1/L2 norm penalties. Including dropout layers after certain layers could help improve generalization capabilities.

4) Adjusting learning rate schedules: Optimizing the learning rate schedule is crucial for achieving good convergence rates. Exponential decay schedules often perform well especially when combined with early stopping criteria. However, linear decay schedules sometimes outperform them under certain circumstances.

5) Normalizing inputs/outputs: Normalizing inputs and outputs can improve stability and robustness of deep learning models. Common normalization methods include z-score normalization, batch normalization, and layer-wise normalization. These techniques ensure that all inputs receive similar treatment regardless of their scale.

6) Regularizing weights: Weight regularization techniques such as weight decay penalize large weights during backpropagation. This encourages small weights leading to more stable gradients and improved convergence properties.

7) Using data augmentation techniques: Data augmentation involves applying transformations to training samples before feeding them into the network. Examples include flipping images horizontally, rotating them randomly, adding noise, etc. These techniques increase the diversity of examples seen by the network thereby improving its ability to generalize across unseen situations.

8) Fine tuning pre-trained models: Pre-trained models trained on large datasets provide strong initializations for downstream tasks. Fine-tuning refers to adapting these pretrained models to new domains or tasks. It involves adjusting the final few layers of the network to fit the particular problem domain.

9) Combining multiple architectures: Combining multiple architectures together can lead to significant improvements in performance. For example, combining ResNet blocks with DenseNets yields state-of-the-art results on many vision tasks.

10) Implementing parallelism: Parallel computing refers to running multiple copies of the same program simultaneously on separate processors or cores. This technique can speed up computations significantly especially when dealing with large datasets or complex algorithms.

11) Utilizing GPUs: Graphics Processing Units (GPUs) offer tremendous computational power compared to CPUs making them ideal candidates for machine learning applications requiring high throughput. GPU acceleration can significantly reduce training time and memory requirements.

12) Leveraging cloud services: Cloud computing platforms provide access to powerful servers located around the world allowing users to run their programs remotely. This eliminates the need to purchase expensive hardware and frees up valuable resources within organizations.

13) Employing transfer learning: Transfer learning involves reusing knowledge acquired during training on one task to solve related problems. This approach saves considerable amounts of time since only a subset of the entire dataset needs to be used instead of starting from scratch.

