 about how we could achieve this goal. First of all, let us try to understand what is happening inside the `MinPool2d_y` class. We know that it takes two arguments: `ks`, which represents the kernel size, and `ceil_mode`. The first argument determines the shape of the pooling window, while the second one controls whether or not the output will be rounded up to the nearest integer value. So far so good! Now let us look at the `forward()` method. It simply applies the negative sign to each element of the input tensor before passing it through the max pooling layer. This way, we end up getting the maximum absolute value within the specified region. Got it? Great! Moving on... 

Now, let us focus our attention on the `FCT` class. As you might have guessed already, it stands for Feature Channel Transformation. Its main purpose is to transform the feature channels of the input image. To do so, it uses three different convolutional layers: one for downsampling, another for upsampling, and finally, a third one for combining both results. Additionally, there is also a batch normalization layer included to help stabilize the training process. Cool, right? But wait, there’s more! The last line of the code block contains a call to the `torch.nn.functional.relu()`. This function ensures that only positive values remain after applying the activation function. Why is this important? Well, because if we were to use the original version of the `minpooling` operation without applying any sort of non-linearity, then our model would suffer from vanishing gradients during backpropagation. By adding this simple step, we ensure that our network learns properly even when dealing with very small inputs. And that concludes our discussion regarding the architecture of the proposed solution.

