1. We can add a new parameter `stride` to our `MinPool2d_x` class which will allow us to specify the stride size when performing max pooling operation. This way we can control how much overlap there is between adjacent patches during pooling process and thus affect the amount of information lost due to downsampling. 

2. Next let's modify the implementation of `forward()` method inside `EVE` class so that it takes into account both maximum and minimum values computed from each patch separately before concatenating them together as one feature map. By doing this we ensure that even if some parts of image contain very small negative numbers they won't get discarded completely because of low absolute value threshold set for min pooling layer. 

3. Finally we need to adjust the number of channels passed through convolutional layers since now we have two separate inputs coming from max and min pools respectively. So instead of passing just single channel input we'll pass two channels i.e., first channel corresponds to output of max pool while second channel represents result obtained after applying min pool.

