1. **Retain original class names:** This is a good idea because it helps maintain consistency and makes it easier for others to understand what the code does without having to read through all the details. However, if you want to change something about how the code works, then changing the name might not be necessary as long as you keep the functionality intact.

2. **Format the code in Markdown:** Markdown is a lightweight markup language that allows you to format text using simple syntax. It's often used when writing documentation or creating web pages, but it can also be useful for organizing code snippets into logical blocks. By formatting your code in markdown, you'll make it easier for people who are reading your code to quickly scan through it and find what they need without having to scroll through lines of unformatted text.

3. **Exclude setup/demonstration code:** Setup code is any code that sets up the environment before running the main part of the program. For example, this could include importing libraries, defining variables, etc. Demonstration code is similar but focuses on showing how the program works rather than setting up the environment. Both types of code can be helpful but should only be included if they're relevant to understanding how the program works.

4. **Retain original tensor input/output sizes:** Tensor sizes refer to the number of elements in each dimension of a tensor (e.g., width, height, depth). Keeping these values consistent across different parts of the code ensures that the output will always have the same shape as the input regardless of where it comes from. This can help prevent errors caused by mismatched dimensions between inputs and outputs.

5. **Provide full code:** Providing full code means including everything needed to run the program correctly. This includes both the actual code itself as well as any supporting files such as images, videos, etc. That way anyone who wants to use the code has everything they need to get started right away without having to search for additional resources elsewhere online.

6. **Must function with default settings:** Finally, make sure that the modified version still functions properly with default settings. If there are changes made to the code that affect its behavior under certain conditions, those changes must also be reflected in the new version so that users know exactly what to expect when running the program.

In conclusion, following these steps will ensure that your modified code remains easy to read, understand, and use while still functioning correctly with default settings.

